### How does exceptionally() work?

exceptionally() catches exceptions in the pipeline and returns a recovery value. It is only called when the previous stage completed with an error. On success, it is skipped entirely.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class ExceptionallyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Success case: exceptionally NOT called
        String success = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> "SETTLED:TRD-001")
            .exceptionally(ex -> "FAILED:UNKNOWN")
            .join();
        System.out.println(success); // SETTLED:TRD-001

        // Failure case: exceptionally returns recovery value
        String failure = CompletableFuture.<String>supplyAsync(() -> {
                throw new RuntimeException("DB connection failed");
            })
            .exceptionally(ex -> {
                System.out.println("Recovering: " + ex.getMessage());
                return "FAILED:TRD-001";
            })
            .join();
        System.out.println(failure); // FAILED:TRD-001
    }
}
```

```text
SETTLED:TRD-001
Recovering: java.lang.RuntimeException: DB connection failed
FAILED:TRD-001
```

### How does handle() work?

handle() is called on both success and failure. It receives the result (or null if failed) and the exception (or null if succeeded). It returns a new value, transforming either case.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class HandleDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CompletableFuture<String> status = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> {
                if (Math.random() > 0.5)
                    throw new RuntimeException("Settlement failed");
                return "SETTLED:TRD-001";
            })
            .handle((result, ex) -> {
                if (ex != null) {
                    return "ERROR: " + ex.getCause().getMessage();
                }
                return "OK: " + result;
            });

        System.out.println(status.join());
    }
}
```

### How does whenComplete() work?

whenComplete() is called on both success and failure for side effects only. It does not change the result. The original value or exception passes through unchanged.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;

public class WhenCompleteDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String result = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> "SETTLED:TRD-001")
            .whenComplete((r, ex) -> {
                if (ex != null) {
                    System.out.println("[AUDIT] Failed: " + ex.getMessage());
                } else {
                    System.out.println("[AUDIT] Success: " + r);
                }
                // No return! Side effect only.
            })
            .join(); // original result passes through

        System.out.println(result); // SETTLED:TRD-001
    }
}
```

```text
[AUDIT] Success: SETTLED:TRD-001
SETTLED:TRD-001
```

### How do the three methods compare?

| Method | Called when | Returns | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| exceptionally | Error only | Recovery T | Recover from error |
| handle | Always | New U | Transform both cases |
| whenComplete | Always | Same T/error | Observe (log/metrics) |

### How do you chain them in a pipeline?

```java
CompletableFuture<String> result = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> settleTradeOrThrow("TRD-001"))  // may throw
    .thenApply(r -> "Confirmed: " + r)                  // transform
    .exceptionally(ex -> "Pending: retry later")         // recover
    .whenComplete((r, ex) -> {                           // log
        System.out.println("[METRIC] outcome=" + r);
    });
```

On success: thenApply transforms, exceptionally is skipped, whenComplete logs.
On failure: thenApply is skipped, exceptionally provides a fallback, whenComplete logs the fallback.

### What is the wrong way to handle errors?

```java
// WRONG: using whenComplete to recover
cf.whenComplete((r, ex) -> {
    if (ex != null) return "fallback"; // DOES NOTHING! No return value.
});
// whenComplete cannot change the result. Use exceptionally or handle.

// WRONG: swallowing exception in handle without logging
cf.handle((r, ex) -> {
    if (ex != null) return defaultValue; // silently swallowed!
    return r;
});
// At least log the error before recovering.
```

### What about exceptionallyCompose (Java 12)?

exceptionallyCompose() allows async recovery by returning a CompletableFuture instead of a plain value.

```java
// Java 12+
CompletableFuture<Integer> score = fetchCreditScore("SAV2001")
    .exceptionallyCompose(ex -> fetchCachedScoreAsync("SAV2001"));
// If live score fails, fetch cached score asynchronously
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Does exceptionally recover the pipeline for subsequent stages? Yes, the next thenApply receives the recovery value.
- Can handle() change the return type? Yes, handle returns BiFunction<T, Throwable, U> where U can be different from T.
- Is whenComplete called if exceptionally already recovered? If whenComplete is chained after exceptionally, it sees the recovered value, not the original error.
